A||N||8||TwisT||Fiero body-prep basics;  Which grits to use?||||1||
Z||000000||TwisT||04-14-2001||01:08 PM||||Hey gang...<P>I'm now prepared to begin the paint-prep on my '88 GT and am seeking some body-prep basics.<P>First thing I'm doing is sanding.  What grits are recommended,  start to finish?  I will be using your basic orbital sander (electric).<P>Thanks for any info...<P>Brett||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||04-14-2001||03:23 PM||||If all you are doing is scuffing finish then 800-1000 and feathering chips start at 600-800 and go finer from there. You'll be suprised how fast that grit cuts on an electris sander. <P>for scuffing I'd try hitting it with wet 1200-1500 and see what you get just with hand sanding it. It will go allot faster than you think. All you are trying to do is remove the top oxide layers, fine scratches and chips.<P>If you have to use filler on big chips that penetrate the panel then start at 220-400 just to even up the filler. keep the filler to a minimum. (The hood often gets big rock dings in the pannel.)<P>There's no area where you'd normally want any more coarse than that. If you dig big scratches you'll have a big pain getting them cleaned up. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||danny boy||04-14-2001||04:54 PM|||| I just went over my whole car with 60 grit to get the 10 layers of paint/ primer/ clearcoat off. then I went straight to 220. With the 60 it took me 2 hrs to do the whole car, then about 9 with the 220 to fix everything I messed up with the 60. <BR>My car had been painted several times and each time all that was done was scuffing the clearcoat. On cars that aren't in this bad shape, I usually use 180 to start. If you are new to bodywork, start with at least 220. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||04-14-2001||09:30 PM||||In my shop I use 80 grit to knock out bad chips and scratches, body work filler. Then dress it up with 180. Run over all general surface with 320 on a DA. Finish sand rest and primered areas with 400. Red scotchbrite areas around mouldings, seams. After its painted wet sand with 1000, then 1500 to rub with superduty compound, then swirl remover. Then looks like this. <P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=9116379&p=34556781"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||04-15-2001||12:11 AM||||a short thought...<P>it's better to start with fine paper and work slow then to use coarse stuff and have to fart with cleaning up after messing up.<P>It also depends how the car is to start with. If you are dealing with the OE paint then that's one thing. If you have layers of bad paint then you have more work ahead.<P>The main thing to remember is that Fiero body pannels are not much harder than the paint. Very easy to dig major holes in them.<P>Even with layers of paint.... I would not start higher than maybe 120 grit.<P>Another thing... buy good paper.  cheap paper will give the same result. Sand paper is definatly one of those places you get what you pay for.<P>Myself I'll only use wet sanding with Aluminum Oxide paper from someone like 3M. Wet paper won't load up like it will dry. you get allot more mileage out of the paper and a much better finish for the new paint.||||reg||1
Z||000005||mgbringo||04-15-2001||01:20 PM||||It really depends on how many coats you have and what shape they are in.  If it is all original and in good shape, no cracking, etc, just scuff it up with 220 and then 400 or 600.  Leave the original base there for the new paint.<P>If it has many layers of re-paints then use 80 and work on up to 600 before you lay down new primer/paint.<P>I recommend NOT using an electric orbital sander.  It can leave little circles in the finish that will show through.  Use a DA sander or by hand.  Be sure to use a rubber block when you do it by hand.  <P>Good Luck.<P>------------------<BR>1986 Fiero GT<p>[This message has been edited by mgbringo (edited 04-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||TwisT||04-15-2001||02:00 PM||||I think I goofed...  what's a "DA" sander?<P>If it is what I think it is,  then a "DA" sander is what I'm using.  Isn't an orbital sander one which just spins?  This one isn't.  It spins also,  but at a variable "vibrating" fashion.  It's the one that has holes in the disk for the sander to "suck" out the dust while sanding...<P>Anyway...  LOL...  My Fiero HAS been painted once over the factory paint (factory white,  then aftermarket white).  I also have a couple of replacement panels that are factory colored.<P>Brett||||reg||1
Z||000007||Cooter||04-15-2001||02:04 PM||||Another quick/easy paint prep is if the original finish is pretty good, use a DA with 400 on all the flat areas, then go back and wet sand with 400 to get around all the little places and edges. When sanding the old paint off with course paper, like 80, do not cut all the way down to the plastic, leave some paint on the surface. Then you can feather edge out the sanding marks with some 180-220 and not have to worry about cutting into the plastic too much.||||reg||1
Z||000008||mgbringo||04-15-2001||03:03 PM||||I also agree with Cooter.  That base primer from the factory is usually a very good base to work from.  <P>A DA sander is dual action sander.  They are air powered off of your compressor. <P>Another important issue is to make sure all the putty/primer/paint is compatible.  Good idea to use a primer/sealer over the base and then use compatible products from there from one manufacturer.<P>------------------<BR>1986 Fiero GT||||reg||1
